==English==

===Etymology===
{{etyl|enm}} ''[[brawne#Middle English|brawne]]'' from {{etyl|fro}} ''[[braon#Old French|braon]]'' "slice of meat, fleshy part, buttock", of {{etyl|gem}} origin, from {{etyl|frk}} *{{term||brādon}}, accusative form of *''{{term||brādo|roasted meat, ham}} from {{proto|Germanic|brǣdon|meat, roast|lang=en}} from {{proto|Indo-European|bhre-|to burn, heat|lang=en}} from {{proto|Indo-European|bureue-|to boil, bubble, burn|lang=en}}. Akin to {{etyl|goh|-}} ''[[brato#Old High German|brāto]]'' "tender meat" ({{etyl|deu|-}} ''[[Braten#German|Braten]]'' "roast"), {{etyl|ang|-}} ''[[bræd#Old English|brǣd]]'' "flesh, meat", {{etyl|non|-}} ''[[bráð#Old Norse|brāð]]'' "raw meat".

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|ɔːn}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}
{{wikipedia|Head cheese|brawn}}

# Strong [[muscle]]s or [[lean]] [[flesh]], especially of the arm, leg or thumb.
# Physical [[strength]]; [[muscularity]].
# {{context|mostly|British}} [[head cheese]]; a [[terrine]] made from the [[head]] of a [[pig]] or [[calf]]; originally [[boar]]'s meat.

